How can I use Scala code to generate a list of prime numbers up to a given limit?
Here's an example of how you can do it:
```
object PrimeNumbers {
  def main(args: Array[String]) {
    val limit = 100
    val primes = getPrimes(limit)
    println("Prime numbers up to " + limit + ":")
    primes.foreach(println)
  }
  def getPrimes(limit: Int): List[Int] = {
    val sieve = Array.fill(limit + 1)(true)
    sieve(0) = false
    sieve(1) = false
    for (i <- 2 to math.sqrt(limit).toInt) {
      if (sieve(i)) {
        for (j <- i * i to limit by i) {
          sieve(j) = false
        }
      }
    }
    sieve.indices.filter(sieve).toList
  }
}
```
This code uses the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm to generate a list of prime numbers up to a given limit. The `getPrimes` function takes an integer `limit` as input and returns a list of all prime numbers up to that limit. The `main` function sets the limit to 100 and prints out the resulting list of primes. You can customize the limit to suit your needs.

