Edge computing is a way of processing data closer to the source, rather than sending all data to a centralized cloud server for processing. This approach can help reduce latency, improve data privacy, and reduce network bandwidth requirements. 
In Scala, one way to implement edge computing is by using Akka cluster. Akka is a toolkit and runtime for building highly concurrent, distributed, and fault-tolerant systems. Akka cluster allows you to create a cluster of Akka nodes that can communicate with each other and share workloads. 
Here's an example of using Akka cluster for edge computing:
```scala
import akka.actor.{Actor, ActorSystem, Props}
import akka.cluster.Cluster
import akka.cluster.routing.{ClusterRouterPool, ClusterRouterPoolSettings}
import akka.routing.BroadcastPool
class EdgeNode extends Actor {
  def receive = {
    case data: String =>
      // Process data locally
      val result = processData(data)

      // Send result back to sender
      sender() ! result
  }

  def processData(data: String): String = {
    // TODO: Implement data processing logic
    "Processed " + data
  }
}
object EdgeNode {
  def main(args: Array[String]): Unit = {
    val system = ActorSystem("EdgeSystem")
    val cluster = Cluster(system)

    // Join Akka cluster
    cluster.join(cluster.selfAddress)

    // Create edge node actor
    val edgeNode = system.actorOf(Props[EdgeNode], "edgeNode")

    // Create cluster router to broadcast messages to all edge nodes
    val router = system.actorOf(
      ClusterRouterPool(
        BroadcastPool(10),
        ClusterRouterPoolSettings(
          totalInstances = 100,
          maxInstancesPerNode = 10,
          allowLocalRoutees = true,
          useRole = None
        )
      ).props(Props[EdgeNode]),
      name = "router"
    )

    // Send data to edge nodes for processing
    router ! "Data to be processed"
  }
}
```
In this example, we create an Akka actor system and join an Akka cluster. We then create an edge node actor that receives data and processes it locally. We also create a cluster router that broadcasts messages to all edge nodes in the cluster. Finally, we send data to the cluster router for processing.
The benefits of using Akka for edge computing compared to other frameworks include its fault-tolerance, scalability, and distributed processing capabilities. Akka also provides a high-level API for building distributed systems, making it easier to develop and maintain complex applications.
In healthcare and finance, edge computing can be utilized for real-time data processing. For example, in healthcare, edge computing can be used to process patient data in real-time, allowing doctors to make faster and more informed decisions. In finance, edge computing can be used to process financial data in real-time, enabling faster trading and risk analysis. Edge computing can also help ensure data privacy and security by keeping sensitive data closer to the source.

